    expectedAnswer: "N-Queens places n queens on an n×n board so that no two queens share a row, column, or diagonal. Backtracking places one queen per row and prunes any column or diagonal already occupied.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Track columns and both diagonal families.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction solveNQueens(n: number): string[][] {\n  const board: string[][] =\n    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(\".\"));\n\n  const columns = new Set<number>();\n  const diagonals = new Set<number>();      // row - col\n  const antiDiagonals = new Set<number>();  // row + col\n  const result: string[][] = [];\n\n  function backtrack(row: number): void {\n    if (row === n) {\n      result.push(board.map((line) => line.join(\"\")));\n      return;\n    }\n\n    for (let col = 0; col < n; col++) {\n      const diagonal = row - col;\n      const antiDiagonal = row + col;\n\n      if (columns.has(col)) continue;\n      if (diagonals.has(diagonal)) continue;\n      if (antiDiagonals.has(antiDiagonal)) continue;\n\n      columns.add(col);\n      diagonals.add(diagonal);\n      antiDiagonals.add(antiDiagonal);\n      board[row]![col] = \"Q\";\n\n      backtrack(row + 1);\n\n      board[row]![col] = \".\";\n      columns.delete(col);\n      diagonals.delete(diagonal);\n      antiDiagonals.delete(antiDiagonal);\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (n > 0) backtrack(0);\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — WITH BUILT-IN:\nThe implementation above already uses JavaScript Set operations as the practical built-in alternative. The key optimization is constant-time membership checks for columns and diagonals.\n\nStep 3 — Example:\nn=4 has 2 valid solutions.\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nWorst-case search is exponential, commonly described around O(n!) search growth, with O(n²) board output/state plus O(n) sets.\nThe pruning removes many impossible branches.",
